    "evidence_text": "Proinflammatory cytokines underlying the inflammation of Crohn's disease.\n\nWe demonstrate a complex interplay between IL-2 and inflammatory signals during CTL differentiation.\n\nThe future developments of anti-TNFalpha and anti-cytokine therapy in general will be interesting.\n\nHowever, microglial responses are more persistent due to lack of negative regulation on pro-IL-1\u03b2 expression.\n\nMemory CD4+ T cells induce innate responses independently of pathogen\n\nInflammation induced by recognition of pathogen-associated molecular patterns markedly affects subsequent adaptive responses.\n\nWe asked whether the adaptive immune system can also affect the character and magnitude of innate inflammatory responses.\n\nIL-6 as a keystone cytokine in health and disease\n\nIL-6 has context-dependent pro- and anti-inflammatory properties and is now regarded as a prominent target for clinical intervention.\n\nType 2 cytokines: mechanisms and therapeutic strategies\n\nNevertheless, when type 2 responses are dysregulated, they can become important drivers of disease.\n\nEarly views of autoimmune disease cast IFN\u03b3 as a prototypic pro-inflammatory factor.\n\nNeutrophils are the main effector cells during inflammation, but they can also control excessive inflammatory responses by secreting anti-inflammatory cytokines.\n\nHowever, the mechanisms that modulate their plasticity remain unclear.",
